"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was 3.8, and the surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with pT2, no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymph nodes removed is a factor. The biopsy showed Acinar adenocarcinoma, which aligns with the prostatectomy findings.\n\nCountervailing findings include the positive surgical margins and the absence of lymph nodes removed. The lack of documented postoperative PSA or follow-up significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future disease trajectory. Furthermore, the lack of nodal sampling means the status is pNx, which does not equate to pN0, increasing uncertainty. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
